  "cells": [
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "# 4.01 — Riemannian Metrics\n\nA **Riemannian metric** on a manifold $M$ is a smoothly varying inner product on each tangent space:\n\n$$g_p : T_pM \\times T_pM \\to \\mathbb R, \\qquad \\text{symmetric, positive definite, smooth in } p.$$\n\nIn a chart it is a matrix of functions $g_{ij}(x)$, and the notation everyone actually uses is the **line element**\n\n$$ds^2 = g_{ij}\\,dx^i dx^j .$$\n\nThat is the entire definition, and it is deliberately spare. Compare 3.03, where the first fundamental form was *inherited* — obtained by restricting the dot product of $\\mathbb R^3$ to each tangent plane. Here nothing is inherited. The metric is **postulated**, and there is no ambient space for it to come from.\n\n| Stage 3 (surface in $\\mathbb R^3$) | Stage 4 (abstract manifold) |\n|---|---|\n| $E, F, G$ | $g_{ij}$ |\n| $\\sqrt{EG-F^2}$ | $\\sqrt{\\det g}$ |\n| inherited from $\\mathbb R^3$ | postulated |\n| $n = 2$ only | any $n$ |\n\nEverything computable from $\\mathrm{I}$ alone in 3.03 — lengths, angles, areas — carries over verbatim, because those computations never used the ambient space in the first place. The Theorema Egregium (3.06) is the licence: it showed that even curvature survives the amputation.\n\n**What the abstraction buys.** Two things this notebook demonstrates concretely:\n\n- **Dimension is unrestricted.** Nothing above mentions $2$ or $3$. Stage 6 needs $n = 4$ with a sign flipped.\n- **Spaces with no isometric embedding become ordinary.** The **flat torus** — genuinely flat, $K \\equiv 0$, and compact — cannot sit isometrically in $\\mathbb R^3$, yet is perfectly well defined as a manifold with a metric. The embedded torus of 3.04 was a *different* geometric object that merely happened to share its topology.",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "load(\"numerics\")$\nload(\"ax-plots\")$\nload(\"linearalgebra\")$\nratprint : false$\nts(e) := trigsimp(e)$\n\n/* A metric is just a symmetric positive-definite matrix of functions. */\n/* Length of a curve, and the volume element, from g alone. */\ncurve_length(g, vars, curve, prm, t0, t1) := block([sub, vel, integrand],\n  sub : map(lambda([k], vars[k] = curve[k]), makelist(k,k,1,length(vars))),\n  vel : map(lambda([c], diff(c, prm)), curve),\n  integrand : sum( sum( subst(sub, g[i][j])*vel[i]*vel[j], j,1,length(vars)),\n                   i,1,length(vars)),\n  integrate( sqrt(ratsimp(integrand)), prm, t0, t1) )$\n\nvol_element(g) := ts( sqrt(determinant(g)) )$\n\n/* ---------------- four metrics ---------------- */\nassume(R > 0, y > 0)$\n\ng_flat  : matrix([1,0],[0,1])$                       /* Euclidean plane        */\ng_sph   : matrix([R^2,0],[0,R^2*sin(u)^2])$          /* round sphere, radius R */\ng_hyp   : matrix([1/y^2,0],[0,1/y^2])$               /* hyperbolic half-plane  */\ng_ftor  : matrix([1,0],[0,1])$                       /* flat torus (see below) */\n\nprint(\"   metric                  g                          √det g\")$\nprint(\"   Euclidean plane   \", g_flat, \"      \", vol_element(g_flat))$\nprint(\"   round sphere      \", g_sph,  \"  \", vol_element(g_sph))$\nprint(\"   hyperbolic plane  \", g_hyp,  \"        \", vol_element(g_hyp))$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"The hyperbolic metric is ds² = (dx² + dy²)/y², i.e. |dz|/Im z —\")$\nprint(\"exactly what 2.06 derived by demanding PSL(2,R) invariance.\")$\nprint(\"There it was justified by a group; here it is simply written down.\")$",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 1,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "name": "stdout",
          "output_type": "stream",
          "text": [
            "   metric                  g                          √det g\n   Euclidean plane    matrix([1,0],[0,1])        1\n   round sphere       matrix([R^2,0],[0,R^2*sin(u)^2])    R^2*abs(sin(u))\n   hyperbolic plane   matrix([1/y^2,0],[0,1/y^2])          1/y^2\nThe hyperbolic metric is ds² = (dx² + dy²)/y², i.e. |dz|/Im z —\nexactly what 2.06 derived by demanding PSL(2,R) invariance.\nThere it was justified by a group; here it is simply written down."
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "/* --- Everything 2.06 computed, recovered from g alone --- */\nprint(\"hyperbolic half-plane, ds² = (dx² + dy²)/y²\")$\n\n/* the vertical segment from i to 4i */\nL1 : curve_length(g_hyp, [x,y], [0, t], t, 1, 4)$\nprint(\"   length of the segment i → 4i : \", L1, \" = \", float(L1))$\nprint(\"   2.06 got d(i,4i) = ln 4 = \", float(log(4)), \" ✓\")$\n\n/* the boundary is infinitely far: length of εi → i is −log ε */\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"   length of the segment εi → i (numerically):\")$\nfor ee in [1/10, 1/100, 1/1000, 1/10000] do\n  print(\"      ε = \", float(ee), \"   length = \",\n        float( curve_length(g_hyp, [x,y], [0, t], t, ee, 1) ),\n        \"     −log ε = \", float(-log(ee)))$\nprint(\"   — diverging, so y = 0 is infinitely far away ✓\")$\n\n/* a horizontal segment high up is short; low down it is long */\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"   horizontal segment from (0,h) to (1,h), for various heights h:\")$\nfor hh in [4, 2, 1, 1/2, 1/10] do\n  print(\"      h = \", float(hh), \"   length = \",\n        float( curve_length(g_hyp, [x,y], [t, hh], t, 0, 1) ))$\nprint(\"   — the same Euclidean segment costs more the closer it lies to y = 0\")$\n\n/* Area of the ideal triangle, from √det g = 1/y² */\nprint(\"\")$\ninner : integrate(1/yv^2, yv, sqrt(1-xv^2), inf)$\nA_ideal : integrate(inner, xv, -1, 1)$\nprint(\"   area of the ideal triangle (|x| ≤ 1, above the unit circle) = \", A_ideal)$\nprint(\"   2.06 got π, and Gauss–Bonnet predicts π − (0+0+0) = π ✓\")$\n\n/* --- The sphere, likewise --- */\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"round sphere of radius R:\")$\nprint(\"   meridian from pole to pole = \",\n      curve_length(g_sph, [u,v], [t, 0], t, 0, %pi))$\nprint(\"   equator                    = \",\n      curve_length(subst(u=%pi/2, g_sph), [u,v], [%pi/2, t], t, 0, 2*%pi))$\nprint(\"   total area = \", integrate(integrate(R^2*sin(u), u, 0, %pi), v, 0, 2*%pi))$",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 4,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "name": "stdout",
          "output_type": "stream",
          "text": [
            "hyperbolic half-plane, ds² = (dx² + dy²)/y²\n   length of the segment i → 4i :  log(4)  =  1.3862943611198906\n   2.06 got d(i,4i) = ln 4 =  1.3862943611198906  ✓\n   length of the segment εi → i (numerically):\n      ε =  0.1    length =  2.302585092994046      −log ε =  2.302585092994046\n      ε =  0.01    length =  4.605170185988092      −log ε =\n          4.605170185988092\n      ε =  0.001    length =  6.907755278982137      −log ε =\n          6.907755278982137\n      ε =  1.0e-4    length =  9.210340371976184      −log ε =\n          9.210340371976184\n   — diverging, so y = 0 is infinitely far away ✓\n   horizontal segment from (0,h) to (1,h), for various heights h:\n      h =  4.0    length =  0.25\n      h =  2.0    length =  0.5\n      h =  1.0    length =  1.0\n      h =  0.5    length =  2.0\n      h =  0.1    length =  10.0\n   — the same Euclidean segment costs more the closer it lies to y = 0\n   area of the ideal triangle (|x| ≤ 1, above the unit circle) =  %pi\n   2.06 got π, and Gauss–Bonnet predicts π − (0+0+0) = π ✓\nround sphere of radius R:\n   meridian from pole to pole =  %pi*R\n   equator                    =  2*%pi*R\n   total area =  4*%pi*R^2"
          ]
