    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "## The flat torus: a metric with no home in $\\mathbb R^3$\n\nTake the square $[0,1]^2$, glue left edge to right and top to bottom, and give it the metric\n\n$$ds^2 = dx^2 + dy^2 .$$\n\nFormally: $T^2 = \\mathbb R^2/\\mathbb Z^2$ with the metric descending from the Euclidean plane, which works because translations are isometries. The result is the **flat torus**:\n\n- **compact**, with no boundary;\n- **flat**: $g_{ij} = \\delta_{ij}$ in every chart, so $\\Gamma^k_{ij} = 0$ and $K \\equiv 0$ *pointwise*;\n- total area $1$;\n- $\\chi = 0$, consistent with Gauss–Bonnet since $\\iint K\\,dA = 0$ trivially.\n\n**Now compare it with the torus of 3.04**, the doughnut sitting in $\\mathbb R^3$. Both are topologically $T^2$. Both satisfy $\\iint K\\,dA = 0$. But for entirely different reasons:\n\n| | flat torus | embedded torus (3.04) |\n|---|---|---|\n| $K$ at a point | $0$ everywhere | varies: $+$ outside, $-$ inside |\n| $\\iint K\\,dA$ | $0$ because the integrand vanishes | $0$ by **cancellation** ($+4\\pi$ and $-4\\pi$) |\n| lives in | no ambient space needed | $\\mathbb R^3$ |\n\nThey are different **geometric** objects with the same topology. Gauss–Bonnet constrains only the total, and both meet the constraint their own way.\n\n**And the flat torus cannot be fixed by embedding it better.** A compact surface in $\\mathbb R^3$ must have a point of *positive* curvature: take the point farthest from the origin, at distance $R$; the surface lies inside the sphere of radius $R$ and touches it there, so it is at least as curved, giving $K \\ge 1/R^2 > 0$. A surface with $K \\equiv 0$ can therefore never be compact in $\\mathbb R^3$.\n\n*(A genuine subtlety: this rules out $C^2$ embeddings. The Nash–Kuiper theorem gives $C^1$ isometric embeddings of the flat torus — so bizarre that one was not visualised until 2012, by the Hévéa project. $C^1$ is too weak for curvature to be defined, which is why there is no contradiction.)*",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "/* --- Flat torus versus embedded torus: same topology, different geometry --- */\n\n/* Intrinsic K from the metric, for an orthogonal g (the 3.06 formula). */\nK_from_metric(g, vars) := block([E_, G_, W, u_, v_],\n  u_ : vars[1], v_ : vars[2],\n  if ts(g[1][2]) # 0 then error(\"this formula assumes g₁₂ = 0\"),\n  E_ : g[1][1], G_ : g[2][2], W : sqrt(E_*G_),\n  ts( -1/(2*W)*( diff( diff(G_,u_)/W, u_ ) + diff( diff(E_,v_)/W, v_ ) ) ) )$\n\nprint(\"FLAT TORUS  R²/Z²,  g = diag(1,1):\")$\nprint(\"   K = \", K_from_metric(g_ftor, [x,y]), \"   everywhere\")$\nprint(\"   area = \", integrate(integrate(vol_element(g_ftor), x, 0, 1), y, 0, 1))$\nprint(\"   ∫∫K dA = 0 because the integrand is identically zero\")$\n\n/* The embedded torus of 3.04, for contrast */\ng_etor : matrix([1, 0], [0, (3 + cos(u))^2])$    /* a = 3, b = 1 */\nKe : K_from_metric(g_etor, [u,v])$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"EMBEDDED TORUS (a=3, b=1),  g = diag(1, (3+cos u)²):\")$\nprint(\"   K = \", Ke, \"   — varies with u\")$\nprint(\"   K at the outer equator (u=0)  = \", subst(u=0, Ke))$\nprint(\"   K at the inner equator (u=π)  = \", subst(u=%pi, Ke))$\nprint(\"   ∫∫K dA = \",\n      integrate(integrate( ratsimp(Ke*(3+cos(u))), u, 0, 2*%pi), v, 0, 2*%pi),\n      \"  — zero by cancellation, not pointwise\")$\n\n/* Same K as computed extrinsically in 3.04? */\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"   (3.04 computed K = cos(u)/(cos(u)+3) extrinsically from II;\")$\nprint(\"    the intrinsic formula above gives \", Ke, \" — the same,\")$\nprint(\"    which is the Theorema Egregium doing its job.)\")$\n\n/* The obstruction, made concrete */\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"why the flat torus cannot be a surface in R³:\")$\nprint(\"   a compact surface has a farthest point, at distance R from the origin;\")$\nprint(\"   there it is at least as curved as the sphere of radius R, so K ≥ 1/R².\")$\nprint(\"   The embedded torus obeys this — its maximum K is \",\n      float(subst(u=0, Ke)), \" > 0.\")$\nprint(\"   The flat torus has K ≡ 0, so no such point exists, so it is not\")$\nprint(\"   a C² surface in R³ at all — though it is a perfectly good manifold.\")$",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 5,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "name": "stdout",
          "output_type": "stream",
          "text": [
            "FLAT TORUS  R²/Z²,  g = diag(1,1):\n   K =  0    everywhere\n   area =  1\n   ∫∫K dA = 0 because the integrand is identically zero\nEMBEDDED TORUS (a=3, b=1),  g = diag(1, (3+cos u)²):\n   K =  cos(u)/(cos(u)+3)    — varies with u\n   K at the outer equator (u=0)  =  1/4\n   K at the inner equator (u=π)  =  -(1/2)\n   ∫∫K dA =  0   — zero by cancellation, not pointwise\n   (3.04 computed K = cos(u)/(cos(u)+3) extrinsically from II;\n    the intrinsic formula above gives  cos(u)/(cos(u)+3)  — the same,\n    which is the Theorema Egregium doing its job.)\nwhy the flat torus cannot be a surface in R³:\n   a compact surface has a farthest point, at distance R from the origin;\n   there it is at least as curved as the sphere of radius R, so K ≥ 1/R².\n   The embedded torus obeys this — its maximum K is  0.25  > 0.\n   The flat torus has K ≡ 0, so no such point exists, so it is not\n   a C² surface in R³ at all — though it is a perfectly good manifold."
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "/* --- The first fundamental form IS a pullback metric --- */\n/* Given F : M → R³, the pullback F*δ has components  g_ij = ∂F/∂xⁱ · ∂F/∂xʲ.\n   That is exactly the definition of E, F, G from 3.03. */\npullback(F_, vars) := block([n_, d],\n  n_ : length(vars),\n  d : makelist( map(lambda([e], diff(e, vars[k])), F_), k, 1, n_),\n  genmatrix( lambda([i,j], ts( d[i] . d[j] )), n_, n_) )$\n\nsphere_emb : [R*sin(u)*cos(v), R*sin(u)*sin(v), R*cos(u)]$\nprint(\"embedding F(u,v) = (R sin u cos v, R sin u sin v, R cos u)\")$\nprint(\"   pullback F*δ  = \", pullback(sphere_emb, [u,v]))$\nprint(\"   the round metric g_sph = \", g_sph)$\nprint(\"   identical ⟹ 3.03's first fundamental form was a pullback metric,\")$\nprint(\"   and Stage 3 was doing Riemannian geometry without saying so.\")$\n\n/* --- Isometries preserve g; conformal maps rescale it --- */\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"ISOMETRY: a map φ with φ*g = g.\")$\nprint(\"   for the hyperbolic half-plane, PSL(2,R) acts by isometries (2.06),\")$\nprint(\"   which is what singled out ds² = (dx²+dy²)/y² in the first place.\")$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"CONFORMAL: φ*g = λ² g for a positive function λ.\")$\nprint(\"   angles preserved, lengths scaled. 3.03 met this as Mercator,\")$\nprint(\"   where the sphere became sin²u (dw² + dv²) — conformally flat\")$\nprint(\"   but not flat, because λ cannot be removed when K ≠ 0.\")$\n\n/* A conformal metric on the disc: the Poincaré disc model of 2.06 */\ng_disc : matrix([4/(1-x^2-y^2)^2, 0], [0, 4/(1-x^2-y^2)^2])$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"Poincaré disc metric g = 4(dx²+dy²)/(1−x²−y²)²:\")$\nprint(\"   √det g = \", ratsimp(vol_element(g_disc)))$\nprint(\"   K = \", ratsimp( K_from_metric(g_disc, [x,y]) ),\n      \"   — constant −1, the hyperbolic plane again ✓\")$\nprint(\"   (2.06 reached this model via the Cayley transform; here it is\")$\nprint(\"    simply another metric, and the curvature confirms they agree.)\")$",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 6,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "name": "stdout",
          "output_type": "stream",
          "text": [
            "embedding F(u,v) = (R sin u cos v, R sin u sin v, R cos u)\n   pullback F*δ  =  matrix([R^2,0],[0,R^2*sin(u)^2])\n   the round metric g_sph =  matrix([R^2,0],[0,R^2*sin(u)^2])\n   identical ⟹ 3.03's first fundamental form was a pullback metric,\n   and Stage 3 was doing Riemannian geometry without saying so.\nISOMETRY: a map φ with φ*g = g.\n   for the hyperbolic half-plane, PSL(2,R) acts by isometries (2.06),\n   which is what singled out ds² = (dx²+dy²)/y² in the first place.\nCONFORMAL: φ*g = λ² g for a positive function λ.\n   angles preserved, lengths scaled. 3.03 met this as Mercator,\n   where the sphere became sin²u (dw² + dv²) — conformally flat\n   but not flat, because λ cannot be removed when K ≠ 0.\nPoincaré disc metric g = 4(dx²+dy²)/(1−x²−y²)²:\n   √det g =  4/(y^4+(2*x^2-2)*y^2+x^4-2*x^2+1)\n   K =  -1    — constant −1, the hyperbolic plane again ✓\n   (2.06 reached this model via the Cayley transform; here it is\n    simply another metric, and the curvature confirms they agree.)"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "/* --- The three model spaces in geodesic polar coordinates --- */\n/* ds² = dr² + f(r)² dθ², where r is distance from a chosen point. */\ng_polar(f_) := matrix([1, 0], [0, f_^2])$\n\nprint(\"   f(r)        metric                      K            circumference 2π f(r)\")$\nfor m in [[sin(rr),  \"sin r \"], [rr, \"r     \"], [sinh(rr), \"sinh r\"]] do\n  block([gm, Km],\n    gm : g_polar(m[1]),\n    Km : ts( K_from_metric(gm, [rr, th]) ),\n    print(\"   \", m[2], \"   \", gm, \"   \", Km, \"        2π·\", m[1]) )$\n\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"K = −f''/f, exactly the surface-of-revolution formula from 3.05:\")$\nprint(\"   f = sin r  → f'' = −f → K = +1\")$\nprint(\"   f = r      → f'' = 0  → K =  0\")$\nprint(\"   f = sinh r → f'' = +f → K = −1\")$\nprint(\"The same ODE that produced the sphere and the pseudosphere.\")$\n\n/* Circumference and area of a geodesic disc of radius r */\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"   r      C(r) spherical   C(r) flat    C(r) hyperbolic\")$\nfor rv in [0.5, 1.0, 1.5, 2.0, 3.0] do\n  print(\"  \", rv, \"     \", float(2*%pi*sin(rv)), \"     \", float(2*%pi*rv),\n        \"     \", float(2*%pi*sinh(rv)))$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"Positive curvature makes circles SHORTER than 2πr, negative curvature\")$\nprint(\"longer — and in the hyperbolic case exponentially so. That comparison\")$\nprint(\"is the whole content of the theorems in 4.06.\")$\n\nrs : makelist(float(k/100), k, 1, 300)$\nplot2d([ [discrete, rs, map(lambda([r_], float(2*%pi*sin(r_))), rs)],\n         [discrete, rs, map(lambda([r_], float(2*%pi*r_)), rs)],\n         [discrete, rs, map(lambda([r_], float(2*%pi*sinh(r_))), rs)] ],\n       [x, 0, 3], [y, 0, 40],\n       [style, [lines,3],[lines,3],[lines,3]],\n       [legend, \"K = +1 : 2π sin r\", \"K = 0 : 2πr\", \"K = −1 : 2π sinh r\"],\n       [xlabel, \"geodesic radius r\"], [ylabel, \"circumference\"],\n       [title, \"curvature is what circles measure\"])$",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 7,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "name": "stdout",
          "output_type": "stream",
          "text": [
            "   f(r)        metric                      K            circumference 2π f(r)\n    sin r      matrix([1,0],[0,sin(rr)^2])     1         2π· sin(rr)\n    r          matrix([1,0],[0,rr^2])     0         2π· rr\n    sinh r     matrix([1,0],[0,sinh(rr)^2])     -1         2π· sinh(rr)\nK = −f''/f, exactly the surface-of-revolution formula from 3.05:\n   f = sin r  → f'' = −f → K = +1\n   f = r      → f'' = 0  → K =  0\n   f = sinh r → f'' = +f → K = −1\nThe same ODE that produced the sphere and the pseudosphere.\n   r      C(r) spherical   C(r) flat    C(r) hyperbolic\n   0.5       3.0123195000445877       3.141592653589793\n  3.2741383671185713\n   1.0       5.287118128162912       6.283185307179586       7.384006872882645\n   1.5       6.267445843815893       9.42477796076938       13.378657387131112\n   2.0       5.713284232087328       12.566370614359172\n  22.78823602577575\n   3.0       0.8866831611908225       18.84955592153876\n  62.94416455306467\nPositive curvature makes circles SHORTER than 2πr, negative curvature\nlonger — and in the hyperbolic case exponentially so. That comparison\nis the whole content of the theorems in 4.06."
          ]
        },
        {
          "data": {
