    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "## Summary\n\n$$ds^2 = g_{ij}\\,dx^i dx^j, \\qquad L(\\gamma) = \\int\\!\\sqrt{g_{ij}\\dot x^i\\dot x^j}\\,dt, \\qquad dV = \\sqrt{\\det g}\\;dx^1\\cdots dx^n$$\n\n| Space | $g$ | $K$ |\n|---|---|---|\n| Euclidean plane | $\\mathrm{diag}(1,1)$ | $0$ |\n| round sphere $R$ | $\\mathrm{diag}(R^2, R^2\\sin^2 u)$ | $1/R^2$ |\n| hyperbolic half-plane | $\\mathrm{diag}(1/y^2, 1/y^2)$ | $-1$ |\n| Poincaré disc | $\\dfrac{4}{(1-x^2-y^2)^2}\\mathrm{diag}(1,1)$ | $-1$ |\n| flat torus $\\mathbb R^2/\\mathbb Z^2$ | $\\mathrm{diag}(1,1)$ | $0$ |\n\nRecovered from the metric alone, with no ambient space anywhere: $d(i,4i) = \\ln 4$ and the ideal triangle's area $\\pi$ (both matching 2.06); the sphere's meridian $\\pi R$, equator $2\\pi R$, area $4\\pi R^2$; and $-\\log\\varepsilon$ divergence confirming the hyperbolic boundary is infinitely far.\n\nThree connections worth keeping:\n\n**The first fundamental form was a pullback metric.** $F^*\\delta$ for the sphere embedding reproduced $\\mathrm{diag}(R^2, R^2\\sin^2u)$ exactly. Stage 3 was doing Riemannian geometry throughout; it simply always obtained its metric by restriction rather than by decree.\n\n**The Poincaré disc gave $K = -1$ on the nose.** 2.06 reached that model through the Cayley transform and an invariance argument; here it is just another metric, and the curvature computation confirms the two routes agree.\n\n**Constant curvature is one ODE.** In geodesic polar form $ds^2 = dr^2 + f(r)^2d\\theta^2$, the intrinsic formula reduces to $K = -f''/f$ — literally the surface-of-revolution equation of 3.05. Its three solutions $\\sin r$, $r$, $\\sinh r$ give $K = +1, 0, -1$, and the circumferences $2\\pi\\sin r$, $2\\pi r$, $2\\pi\\sinh r$. At $r = 3$ those are $0.89$, $18.85$, and $62.94$: **curvature is what circles measure**, and 4.06's comparison theorems are the general form of that observation.\n\n## The flat torus, and what abstraction is for\n\n$\\mathbb R^2/\\mathbb Z^2$ is compact, has $K \\equiv 0$ pointwise, and cannot be a $C^2$ surface in $\\mathbb R^3$ — because a compact surface has a farthest point where $K \\ge 1/R^2 > 0$. The embedded torus of 3.04 obeys that bound ($K_{\\max} = 1/4$) by making $K$ change sign, so that $\\iint K\\,dA = 0$ holds by cancellation rather than pointwise.\n\nSame topology, same total curvature, entirely different geometry — and only one of them fits in space. That is the concrete payoff of dropping the ambient requirement.\n\n## Next\n\n**4.02 — Connections and Covariant Differentiation** asks how to differentiate a vector field on a manifold. The naive $\\partial_i V^j$ is not a tensor, and repairing it produces the Christoffel symbols — which 3.06 already computed from the metric, and which will now be characterised abstractly by the Levi-Civita conditions of metric-compatibility and torsion-freeness.\n\n## References\n\n- ★ Lee, J.M. (2018) *Introduction to Riemannian Manifolds*. Cham: Springer. doi:10.1007/978-3-319-91755-9. Chapter 2 defines Riemannian metrics and works these examples; Chapter 3 covers the constant-curvature model spaces in exactly the geodesic-polar form used here.\n- ★ Callahan, J.J. (2000) *The Geometry of Spacetime*. New York: Springer. doi:10.1007/978-1-4757-6736-0. Chapter 6 for metrics on manifolds without an ambient space, en route to relativity.\n- do Carmo, M.P. (1992) *Riemannian Geometry*. Boston: Birkhäuser. Chapter 1, the classic concise treatment.\n- ★ Naber, G.L. (1997) *Topology, Geometry, and Gauge Fields*. New York: Springer. doi:10.1007/978-1-4757-2742-5.\n\nOn the flat torus and $C^1$ embeddings: Nash, J. (1954) '$C^1$ isometric imbeddings', *Annals of Mathematics*, 60(3), pp. 383–396; Kuiper, N. (1955) 'On $C^1$-isometric imbeddings', *Indagationes Mathematicae*, 17, pp. 545–556. The 2012 visualisation is due to the Hévéa project (Borrelli, Jabrane, Lazarus and Thibert).\n\n---\n*Marked ★ = in the local indexed library (`maths` collection); the rest are standard references given from knowledge, not consulted here. Every code cell in this notebook was executed and its output is the real result; the prose is written from knowledge and checked against these sources where they cover the material.*",
      "metadata": {}
    }
  ]
